Body:
I traced the current V2 boundary and this still looks broader than Shell: `Tool.Context` has no checkpoint/progress capability, while Effect interruption prevents a normal tool result from reaching settlement. The session layer already has replayable `tool.progress` events, and failed tool states can retain prior structured content, but the runner does not connect those pieces.

I propose a bounded design before implementation:

- let a running tool publish a checkpoint through its context;
- persist that checkpoint through the existing progress event path;
- keep interruption as a failed/interrupted terminal state rather than converting it to success;
- project the latest checkpoint into that terminal state, including managed-output references where applicable.

I would validate this with an interruptible Shell fixture that emits output before cancellation, plus a generic tool fixture: both should remain interrupted, retain the latest checkpoint after replay, and preserve explicit removal semantics. I also checked the closed narrow Shell PR #39562; its author closed it specifically in favor of this broader design, and I found no active replacement PR.

Would the core team approve this boundary and assign the issue before I implement it?

I tried to break the doom_loop. I set it to deny and tried to start the task directly or automatically. Everywhere I asked to run the command `ls -la` five times in a row.

Interactive mode: doom_loop works;
Starting from `--auto': doom_loop works;

Then I created a subagent that runs the same command five times in a row.

Interactive mode: doom_loop does not always work;
Starting from `--auto': doom_loop works;

I looked at what was going on. The fact is that the subagent inserts some thoughts between launches (this is all sorts of nonsense like "nothing has changed since last time, but I have to launch again blah blah blah"). And such a scenario leads to a circumvention of the rule - the system does not consider an identical command repeated five times to be something bad, because the agent was doing something in between. The interesting thing is that it's not clear what to do about it. In some scenarios, the agent really needs to execute the same command, drawing conclusions at intermediate stages. For example, monitoring the progress of any processes.

I suppose the main agent could also break the current implementation of `doom_loop` in the way described. It's just that in my case, he honestly ran the team in a row, without trying to draw intermediate conclusions.

If I understood what you wrote correctly, is this exactly what you caught?

This use case is already supported by the community plugin:
https://github.com/yuhp/opencode-models-discovery

It supports automatic model discovery for any configured `@ai-sdk/openai-compatible` provider and injects the discovered models into `provider.<name>.models` during the config hook.

Example:

```json
{
  "plugin": ["opencode-models-discovery@latest"],
  "provider": {
    "lmstudio": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "name": "LM Studio",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "http://127.0.0.1:1234/v1",
        "modelsDiscovery": {
          "enabled": true
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The plugin supports custom model endpoints, timeouts, model filters, provider-specific metadata enrichment, OpenCode `/connect` credentials, and optional persisted caching. Discovery failures leave explicitly configured models intact.

The current limitation is that discovery happens during config initialization, rather than through a continuously running background refresh. Native support in OpenCode would still be useful, but this plugin can serve as a working solution for the startup-time auto-discovery use case today.

The community plugin already supports enriching custom-provider models from models.dev:

https://github.com/yuhp/opencode-models-discovery

Configure:

```json
{
  "plugin": ["opencode-models-discovery@latest"],
  "provider": {
    "custom-provider": {
      "npm": "@ai-sdk/openai-compatible",
      "options": {
        "baseURL": "https://proxy.example.com/v1",
        "modelsDiscovery": {
          "enabled": true,
          "modelInfoFormat": "models.dev"
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

The plugin fetches models.dev metadata and applies matching limits, capabilities, modalities, and other supported OpenCode model fields to discovered models. Explicit provider configuration remains authoritative.
